{"model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "input": [{"role": "user", "content": "ADVERSARIAL REVIEW REQUEST. Claim under review: \"The sign statement below cannot be obtained from the listed exact structure by an elementary argument; it needs asymptotics plus computer assistance.\" Your task is to REFUTE that claim by producing a complete rigorous proof of the sign statement (for all parameters), or, failing that, to identify precisely which additional inequality would suffice and whether it is plausible. Do not give a hopeful sketch labelled as a proof; if you cannot close it, say so and give the sharpest partial result you can actually prove.\n\nSYSTEM. Parameters mu>0, k>k_H:=mu(1+mu), A:=1+mu, lambda:=k/mu.\n  U' = p,  p' = k - mu p - y,  y' = k e^U - y,   x := k e^U.   Equilibrium E=(0,0,k).\nScalar form U''' + A U'' + mu U' + k(e^U - 1) = 0. Let Gamma be any nonconstant periodic orbit, period T, <f> its time average.\n\nPROVED STRUCTURE (take as given).\n(1) Positive T-periodic adjoint ratios eta, zeta along Gamma: eta' = zeta + (mu-1)eta - eta^2, zeta' = x - (1+eta)zeta, eta > max(0,mu-1). Transverse Floquet multipliers m_s = exp(-(1+<eta>)T) < m_t = exp((<eta>-mu)T). Weak exponent lambda_c := <eta> - mu.\n(2) Tangent orthogonality zeta p - eta p' = x - y. Tangent equation p'' = (eta-mu)p' - zeta p. Operator factorisation D^3 + A D^2 + mu D + x = (D+1+eta)(D^2+(mu-eta)D+zeta).\n(3) (zeta+eta+eta^2/2)' = x - f(eta), f(s)=s(s+1)(s+1-mu), f(mu)=k_H, so k=<f(eta)>.\n(4) Convexity gauges, all >0 on Gamma: K := zeta U - eta p - (y-k), K' = -(1+eta)K + k psi(U), psi(U)=U e^U - e^U + 1 >= 0;  W := zeta' p - eta' p', W' = -(1+eta)W + x p^2;  Lambda_E := (y-k) - zeta_E U + eta_E p with Lambda_E' = -gamma_E Lambda_E + k(e^U-1-U), where gamma_E>A solves gamma(gamma-1)(gamma-mu)=k, eta_E=gamma_E-1, zeta_E=eta_E(eta_E+1-mu).\n(5) Geometry: the projection of Gamma to the (U,p) plane is a clockwise, strictly convex Jordan curve, strictly star-shaped about (0,0): Sigma := p^2 - U p' > 0 and Xi := p'^2 - p p'' > 0, with Sigma' = (eta-mu)Sigma + pK and Xi' = (eta-mu)Xi + pW. U and p each have exactly one max and one min per period. At the crest (p=0, U>0): x>y>k. Six-extrema order X_min<Z_min<Y_min<X_max<Z_max<Y_max in chemical variables X=x/k, Z=y/k, Y=1-p/lambda.\n(6) EXACT FORMULA: lambda_c = -<pK/Sigma> = -<pW/Xi>. With kappa:=K/Sigma>0 and the explicit state function eta0 := [U(x-y) - p(y-k)]/Sigma (which has <eta0> = mu exactly), eta = eta0 - p kappa, and kappa solves the scalar Riccati equation\n      kappa' = k psi(U)/Sigma - (1 + 2 eta0 - mu) kappa + p kappa^2.\n    Equivalently, in polar coordinates (U,p)=r(cos th, sin th) with s:=(y-k)/r:  (log r)' = sin th (cos th - mu sin th - s),  th' = -(sin^2 th + mu sin th cos th + s cos th) <0, and\n      lambda_c T = - integral over one turn of (K/r) sin(th) |d th| = - int_{U_min}^{U_max} (kappa_up(U) - kappa_down(U)) dU,\n    where up/down are the rising (p>0) and falling (p<0) arcs.\n(7) The whole global attractor is the graph y=h(U,p) of a convex C^1 function over the closed disc bounded by the outermost projected cycle, grad h=(zeta,-eta); the flow on it is the planar flow u'=v, v'=k-mu v-h(u,v) with divergence eta-mu; E is an unstable focus; K = h(0,0) - [h - u h_u - v h_v] is the Bregman gap, so K/r = d s/d log r (radial derivative of the secant slope s=(h-k)/r), which is >=0 by convexity.\n(8) Moment identities on Gamma: <x>=k, <p>=0, <p'^2>=mu<p^2>, <x p^2>=k_H <p^2>, <(y-k)^2>=k_H<p^2>, <x/y>=1, <y/(lambda-p)>=mu, <eta p><0, <zeta p'><0.\n(9) Strong-stable Floquet vector e_s=(a,-b,-c), a,b,c>0; r:=b/a, s:=c/a satisfy r'=r^2-mu r-s, s'=s r-s-x, <r>=1+<eta>. The weak adjoint is Psi = e^{At} z' x e_s; each component has exactly two zeros per period.\n(10) Proved separately: lambda_c<0 for every cycle when k>=1000 (mu<=1). Near Hopf lambda_c ~ -c(mu) amplitude^2 (first Lyapunov coefficient negative), so any proof must be exact to second order at E. Numerically lambda_c<0 on every cycle found (mu in [1e-3,1e3], rho=k/k_H up to 300), log m_t strictly decreasing in rho, and along the spiral on the attractor the one-turn mean of eta-mu is strictly decreasing in crest amplitude.\n\nSIGN STATEMENT TO PROVE: for every (mu,k) with k>k_H and every nonconstant periodic orbit, <eta> < mu; equivalently int (kappa_up - kappa_down) dU > 0. (This implies the periodic orbit is unique.)\n\nKNOWN DEAD ENDS (do not repeat): Jensen/Kingman convexity gives only lower bounds on <eta>; any Dulac function regular at E fails; monomial weights x^a y^b (lambda-p)^c fail; Massera radial scaling crosses with the sign of p; Zhang-type f/g monotonicity is false on the attractor; the crest return map is monotone but not concave in natural coordinates; every pairing with the weak adjoint gives one rank-one identity whose sign is the unknown.\n\nOutput: (a) verdict: PROOF / PARTIAL / NO; (b) the argument, fully written; (c) if PARTIAL or NO, the single most promising missing inequality, stated precisely, with a reason to believe or doubt it.\n"}]}
